    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A <strong>Cubii under desk elliptical</strong> is easy to buy and easy to ignore. The real question is not whether it works on day one, but whether it is worth using every day for months. This guide walks through what daily use typically looks like, what owners report over weeks and months, and where the benefits start and stop.</p>
<p>The short version: if you sit for most of the day and want to move more without changing your schedule, daily use is worth it. Expect a modest calorie burn, less stiffness and a habit that sticks if you set it up well. Do not expect it to replace walking or a proper workout. Our observations are based on specs and owner reviews of the Cubii JR2, the most popular model.</p>

<h2>What Is a Cubii?</h2>
<p>A Cubii is a compact seated elliptical. You keep it under your desk, sit in your usual chair and pedal in a smooth oval motion. A resistance dial sets how hard it feels, and a small display shows strides, time and estimated calories. Cubii makes several models: the JR2 is the benchmark, the JR2+ adds Bluetooth, and the JR1, Move and GO cover lower prices and smaller spaces.</p>
<p>The motion is low impact. Your feet never leave the pedals, which is why owners with sore knees or limited fitness often find it more comfortable than a pedal bike. Compare the whole lineup in our <a href="/cubii-pro-hd-review/">Cubii Pro review</a>.</p>

<h2>What Daily Use of a Cubii Under Desk Elliptical Looks Like</h2>
<p>Owner reviews follow a fairly predictable arc. Knowing it helps you get through the stage where most people quit.</p>
<h3>Week 1: novelty and a few annoyances</h3>
<p>The first days are fun. You pedal through emails and calls and watch the stride count climb. You also discover the practical problems: the machine creeps forward on a hard floor, your rolling chair drifts back, or your knees touch the underside of the desk. These are the issues that cause returns, and all of them are fixable in an afternoon.</p>
<h3>Weeks 2 to 4: finding a rhythm</h3>
<p>This is where the habit forms or fails. People who stick with it usually settle on a routine such as pedaling during every call, or for ten minutes at the top of each hour. Light resistance while typing and higher resistance while reading or listening is a common pattern. Owners often say it stops feeling like exercise and starts feeling like fidgeting.</p>
<h3>Months 2 to 3: background movement</h3>
<p>By now, daily users tend to report the benefits that matter most to them: fewer stiff legs at the end of the day, less restlessness during long meetings, and a steady weekly total of minutes moved. The stride count becomes less exciting, so a weekly target helps. Our guide to <a href="/what-really-changes-when-you-use-one-daily/">what really changes when you use one daily</a> goes deeper on this phase.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>The quit point:</strong> most abandoned Cubiis are dropped around week two, usually because of sliding, knee clearance or forgetting. Fix the setup in week one and use a reminder, and you are far more likely to still be pedaling in month three.</p></div>

<h2>Cubii Benefits You Can Realistically Expect</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Less time sitting still.</strong> The CDC and WHO recommend at least 150 minutes of moderate activity a week. A Cubii will not cover all of that, but it turns hours of stillness into light movement.</li>
<li><strong>A modest extra calorie burn.</strong> Small, all-day movement is what researchers call NEAT (non-exercise activity thermogenesis), and research associated with the Mayo Clinic links it to meaningful differences in daily energy use.</li>
<li><strong>Looser legs and hips.</strong> Many owners report less stiffness after long workdays.</li>
<li><strong>Low impact.</strong> The smooth motion is gentle on knees and ankles, which suits beginners and older users.</li>
<li><strong>Quiet focus.</strong> Some people find that steady pedaling helps them stay alert during long calls.</li>
</ul>
<p>These line up with the wider research on breaking up sitting time, which we cover in our guide to <a href="/under-desk-elliptical-benefits/">under desk elliptical benefits</a>. Older users get the same benefits at a gentler pace; our <a href="/best-under-desk-elliptical-for-seniors/">best under desk elliptical for seniors</a> list covers models with easier starts and motorized help.</p>
<h3>Will your Cubii pedaling show up as steps?</h3>
<p>Usually not, or only partly. Wrist trackers count arm swing, and your arms barely move while you pedal seated. The Cubii display counts strides, but your watch may ignore them. Log sessions as a workout, or use the JR2+ to sync data through the Cubii app. Our explainer on <a href="/does-under-desk-elliptical-count-as-steps/">whether an under desk elliptical counts as steps</a> shows how to log it fairly, and the <a href="/tools/under-desk-elliptical-steps-calculator/">steps converter</a> gives you an effort-matched number.</p>

<h2>Does the Cubii Help With Circulation?</h2>
<p>It may. Your calf muscles act as a pump for blood returning from your legs, and they work every time you push a pedal. Sitting completely still for hours takes that pump out of the picture, which is why long flights and long workdays leave legs feeling heavy. Regular gentle pedaling keeps those muscles active.</p>
<p>That is a reasonable everyday benefit, not a medical claim. If you have a diagnosed circulation problem, swelling, a history of blood clots or diabetes-related foot issues, talk to your doctor before you rely on a Cubii, and follow their guidance on how much to use it. For people who cannot pedal under their own power, a motorized machine such as the [[amazon:legxercise|LegXercise Ellipse One]] moves your legs for you.</p>

<h2>Is a Cubii a Good Workout?</h2>
<p>It is light to moderate exercise. In the Compendium of Physical Activities, light seated pedaling is roughly 2.0 METs and moderate effort around 2.8 METs, compared with about 1.3 for sitting. Walking at 3 mph is about 3.5 METs. So a Cubii sits between sitting still and a brisk walk.</p>
<p>At the higher resistance settings you will feel your quads, hamstrings and glutes working, but you will rarely get out of breath. If you want a workout that challenges your heart and lungs, a Cubii is not the tool. If you want more movement during hours you would otherwise lose, it is.</p>
<p>Here is how the calories work out for your weight and the minutes you plan to pedal:</p>
