<h2>How to Make Daily Cubii Use Stick</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Fix the slide on day one.</strong> A [[amazon:cubii-mat|Cubii non-slip mat]] keeps the machine in place and protects the floor.</li>
<li><strong>Lock your chair.</strong> [[amazon:caster-cups|Caster cups]] stop a rolling chair drifting back.</li>
<li><strong>Tie it to triggers.</strong> Pedal during every call, every video, or the first ten minutes of each hour.</li>
<li><strong>Use a timer.</strong> Our <a href="/tools/desk-pedal-timer/">desk pedal timer</a> chimes when it is time to pedal or rest.</li>
<li><strong>Track weekly minutes.</strong> A weekly total is more motivating than daily strides.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Who Should Not Bother</h2>
<ul>
<li>You want serious cardio or strength gains. Choose a bike, a walking pad or a gym routine.</li>
<li>Your desk is low and you are tall. Check fit first with the <a href="/tools/desk-clearance-checker/">desk clearance checker</a>.</li>
<li>You want to stand while you move. Cubii models are seated only.</li>
<li>You rarely sit for long. If you are already on your feet most of the day, the extra movement adds little.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you are on the fence, think about where your sitting hours go. Someone at a desk for seven or eight hours has a lot of still time to convert. Someone who sits for two hours a day would get more from a daily walk.</p>

<h2>Verdict: Is Cubii Worth It?</h2>
<p>A Cubii under desk elliptical is worth using every day if you sit for long stretches and want an easy, quiet way to move more. The benefits are real but modest: extra calories, looser legs and a habit that fits into time you already spend at your desk. It is not a substitute for walking or a workout, and it works best alongside them.</p>
<p>For most people, the [[amazon:cubii-jr2|Cubii JR2]] is the right daily machine. Pair it with a mat, a reminder and realistic expectations, and it is likely to still be in use months from now.</p>
